title ALES140 — Lotus's stereo_halve_neg: the inverted half-wave rectifier carried into stereo, completing the rectifier family in stereo
prompt Read REMEMBER and the 1024-round itinerary + six-season double-seat; take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; approve my own design rounds; make progress before checking in; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates; baton prin recur.
think Third lap of this recur turn (after ALES138 stereo_rectify and ALES139 stereo_halve). The ALES139 log named the inverted half-wave (ALES86) as the next crux to complete the rectifier family in stereo. Taking it closes a clean family arc — a satisfying finishing edge, crux-first.
think ALES86 lives in halve_neg.rye: halve_neg(clip, start, count), y = min(x, 0), keep the negative half and silence the positive, the mirror of ALES85's positive half-wave. HalveNegError = {BadRange} only — no gain, no ceiling, and no rail edge (sample_min passes THROUGH untouched, unlike the full-wave rectifier where its magnitude overflowed the positive rail), so no saturate. Validated the span once against the shared length, ran mono halve_neg per channel, reused HalveNegError whole.
think The round's finishing crux is the FAMILY WHOLE: the two half-waves partition the wave, max(x,0) + min(x,0) = x. Proved it directly in stereo — ran ALES139's stereo_halve on one copy of a master and stereo_halve_neg on another, then asserted their sum equals the dry master sample for sample on each channel (including sample_min on the left and 32767 on the right). Also proved the non-positive mirror of ALES139's partition: an out-of-phase master returns partitioned (left·right = 0, disjoint) with sum = −|left| (min(x,0) + min(−x,0) = −|x|), every output non-positive, plus ratio-held, idempotence, span discipline, and BadRange atomicity. Imported stereo_halve.rye for the family-whole test (a first cross-module witness dependency in this stereo run — caught and fixed an initial wrong import of the mono halve.rye). Witness GREEN; tame_style_check bans clean.
obs The stereo rectifier family now stands WHOLE — full-wave collapse (ALES138), positive half-wave partition (ALES139), inverted half-wave partition (ALES140) — and the even corner of the nonlinear class is complete. The image lesson reads across four verbs, cleanly: copy/linear maps PRESERVE the image, not-odd maps BREAK the antisymmetry, the even full-wave COLLAPSES it to mono, and the even+odd half-waves PARTITION it into disjoint one-sided channels that sum back to the dry signal. No custody gate reached: purely local software, two bounded i16 Clips, a self-approved design round. Three rounds landed and sent this turn (ALES138–140); a good finishing edge. The next Lotus lap opens a fresh direction — the center-clip / dead-zone family (ALES87–89) into stereo, or a new mono primitive — a check-in-worthy choice of heading rather than a mechanical continuation.
loom witness=ales_stereo_halve_neg_witness result=GREEN laws=4 channels=2 class=nonlinear axis=even-odd cases=family-whole+image-partitioned+non-positive+ratio-held+idempotence
file lotus/stereo_halve_neg.rye ALES140 — stereo_halve_neg, the inverted half-wave rectifier lifted to stereo: one shared threshold at zero on both channels, validated once (span only, no saturate owed), four laws with the family-whole law (the two stereo half-waves reconstruct the dry master, max(x,0)+min(x,0)=x) and the non-positive image-partition.
file tools/ales_stereo_halve_neg_witness.rish the witness — builds, runs selftest, asserts the GREEN ales-stereo-halve-neg line.
file active-designing/20260815-041250_fill-ales140-lotus-stereo-inverted-half-wave.md the self-approved design read for this rung.
file lotus/README.md front door synced — ALES140 roster entry on the Status line, stamp refreshed.
recommend check-in (either) the stereo rectifier family stands whole (ALES138–140) and the even corner of the nonlinear class is complete; the next Lotus lap chooses a fresh direction (the center-clip / dead-zone family ALES87–89 into stereo, or a new mono primitive) rather than a mechanical continuation — a good place to confirm heading. Three rounds landed and sent this turn; no gate reached.
